---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Engineering — A3S Labs" description="How A3S Labs approaches application platforms, data systems, infrastructure, deployment and real-time software.">
  <section class="page-hero section-pad">
    <div class="shell page-hero-grid">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span>ENGINEERING</span><span>A3S / 03</span></p>
      <div><h1>Build the whole path.</h1><p>Interface quality matters, but so do ingestion, data models, background work, access boundaries, failure recovery and deployment behavior. A3S Labs engineering work is strongest when those layers are designed as one product.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad section-rule">
    <div class="shell engineering-deep-grid">
      <article>
        <span>01 / APPLICATION PLATFORMS</span>
        <h2>UI and API should share a product model.</h2>
        <p>Next.js, Vue, Android and browser-native interfaces sit on top of typed APIs and persistent domain models. Public identifiers, URL state, server-side credential boundaries and responsive information architecture are treated as application behavior rather than incidental frontend work.</p>
        <div class="tag-row"><span>TypeScript</span><span>Next.js</span><span>Vue</span><span>Android</span><span>FastAPI</span></div>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span>02 / DATA + INTELLIGENCE</span>
        <h2>Preserve evidence through the model layer.</h2>
        <p>The Torque keeps seller/source provenance alongside multimodal extraction; BeeUnity keeps calibration and contextual signals visible. The common rule is that model output should not erase where the evidence came from or pretend uncertainty is ground truth.</p>
        <div class="tag-row"><span>Python</span><span>Gemini</span><span>TensorFlow</span><span>PyTorch</span><span>PostgreSQL</span></div>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span>03 / INFRASTRUCTURE</span>
        <h2>Public routes stay narrow; operator power stays bounded.</h2>
        <p>Containerized services, VPS backends, Cloudflare tunnels and same-origin server proxies are used to avoid exposing privileged control surfaces directly to browsers. Authentication, service identity and role-gated writes are separate concerns.</p>
        <div class="tag-row"><span>Docker</span><span>Cloudflare Access</span><span>Linux</span><span>Robyn</span><span>Vercel</span></div>
        <a href="/case-studies/istatus">iStatus case study ↗</a>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span>04 / REAL-TIME SYSTEMS</span>
        <h2>Simulation logic cannot be a visual side effect.</h2>
        <p>RotorFrontier separates flight state from rendering, runs a deterministic update loop, streams georeferenced terrain and combines AI, weapons, input, audio and peer networking inside a browser runtime.</p>
        <div class="tag-row"><span>Babylon.js</span><span>60 Hz</span><span>WebRTC</span><span>Web Audio</span><span>terrain</span></div>
        <a href="/case-studies/rotorfrontier">RotorFrontier case study ↗</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad detail-band">
    <div class="shell">
      <header class="section-heading split-heading">
        <div><p class="section-kicker">Engineering principles</p><h2>Defaults that survive growth.</h2></div>
        <p>The stack can change. The rules below are intended to survive a framework change, a larger dataset or a more complicated production environment.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="method-strip">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Evidence</h3><p>Expose provenance, source state and uncertainty whenever software interprets external data.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Boundaries</h3><p>Keep secrets server-side, narrow public routes and separate administrative controls from public product surfaces.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Recovery</h3><p>Advance cursors, jobs and state only when work is safely persisted; make transient failures retryable without corrupting identities.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Product quality</h3><p>Typography, accessibility, performance, mobile behavior and deployment correctness belong inside engineering, not after it.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad section-rule">
    <div class="shell narrative-grid">
      <div><p class="section-kicker">A concrete example</p><h2>One vehicle listing crosses nearly every layer.</h2></div>
      <div class="narrative-copy">
        <p>A dealer post can enter through the X API, be associated with an account-specific cursor, recover additional same-dealer thread context, pass through multimodal enrichment, produce structured PostgreSQL records, remain addressable through a stable opaque public ID, travel through a restricted FastAPI/Vercel proxy boundary and finally become a responsive marketplace card or detail view. That whole path is one feature.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/case-studies/the-torque">Read The Torque case study ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad capability-band">
    <div class="shell build-note-grid">
      <div><p class="section-kicker">Deployment discipline</p><h2>Production deploys should be deliberate.</h2></div>
      <div>
        <p>The A3S Labs corporate site itself now uses GitHub Actions for branch/PR build validation while Vercel is reserved for the production branch. That keeps static-site review checks available without spending Vercel resources on automatic preview builds.</p>
        <a class="button button-quiet" href="/builds">Open build log</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
